WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:01.000
So picture this.

00:01.840 --> 00:05.440
You've finally got an evening completely to yourself.

00:05.540 --> 00:06.980
Oh, the absolute dream.

00:07.140 --> 00:07.360
Right.

00:07.720 --> 00:08.640
It's rare.

00:08.880 --> 00:10.200
But you're sitting down on your couch.

00:10.720 --> 00:16.220
You've queued up that highly anticipated action thriller that, you know, everyone has been talking about for months.

00:16.240 --> 00:17.920
Yeah, the big blockbuster.

00:17.920 --> 00:18.320
Exactly.

00:18.820 --> 00:21.780
And you settle in to just lose yourself in the story.

00:22.400 --> 00:28.720
But, like, ten minutes into the film, your posture has completely changed.

00:28.840 --> 00:29.960
You're not relaxed anymore.

00:30.240 --> 00:31.420
No, not at all.

00:31.860 --> 00:36.540
You are leaning forward, and you're desperately clutching the remote control in your hand.

00:36.620 --> 00:37.480
Like it's a lifeline.

00:37.980 --> 00:38.340
Yes.

00:38.680 --> 00:43.860
Your thumb is just hovering over the volume button, riding it like you're trying to tame a wild animal or something.

00:43.940 --> 00:45.340
I know exactly what you mean.

00:45.340 --> 00:53.120
Because when the characters are whispering in a dark room, dropping, you know, critical plot points, you're cranking the volume up just to understand them.

00:53.180 --> 00:54.560
Just to hear a single word.

00:54.720 --> 00:54.960
Right.

00:54.960 --> 01:01.780
But then, a millisecond later, the scene cuts, a car explodes, a gun goes off, and you are scrambling.

01:01.880 --> 01:07.080
You're frantically mashing the volume down button before your walls literally start shaking.

01:07.080 --> 01:09.280
And before your neighbors call the police.

01:09.400 --> 01:09.800
Exactly.

01:10.380 --> 01:13.460
And it just, uh, it completely pulls you out of the movie.

01:13.680 --> 01:14.940
I mean, we've all been there, right?

01:14.940 --> 01:18.060
It's an incredibly frustrating way to spend a Friday night.

01:18.060 --> 01:20.180
It absolutely shatters the immersion.

01:20.420 --> 01:24.220
I mean, instead of simply watching and experiencing the film, you're managing it.

01:24.460 --> 01:25.520
Yeah, that's a good way to put it.

01:25.620 --> 01:26.280
Managing it.

01:26.380 --> 01:26.580
Right.

01:26.720 --> 01:35.180
You've become this active, stressed out participant in a process that is, well, it's supposed to be passive, relaxing, entertaining.

01:35.560 --> 01:36.700
But instead of doing a job.

01:36.700 --> 01:37.300
Exactly.

01:37.460 --> 01:40.700
You're basically acting as a live audio engineer for your own living room.

01:41.360 --> 01:52.800
And, uh, what is so fascinating about this phenomenon is that the film and broadcast industry has historically treated this as a very simple, linear problem.

01:52.960 --> 01:53.300
Really?

01:53.560 --> 01:54.060
Simple how?

01:54.560 --> 02:01.880
Well, they receive thousands of complaints from home viewers, and their core assumption is just, you know, they say, well, the movie is simply too loud.

02:02.120 --> 02:03.800
That feels like a massive understatement.

02:03.800 --> 02:16.640
It is, because the reality of what is actually happening to your ears, to your nervous system, and to your brain in that living room is, uh, it's vastly more complicated than just a high decibel number.

02:16.740 --> 02:20.620
Okay, so let's unpack this, because that brings us to our mission for this deep dive today.

02:20.860 --> 02:27.080
We are looking at a brilliantly detailed 2026 working manuscript.

02:27.340 --> 02:30.220
Yeah, by an independent researcher named Flixtreon.

02:30.220 --> 02:37.680
Right, and it's titled, Acoustic Occupation, Salience, Recovery, and the Temporal Organization of Film Sound.

02:38.100 --> 02:39.360
A very academic title.

02:39.600 --> 02:39.880
Very.

02:40.240 --> 02:47.420
But we are going to use this paper to uncover exactly why modern film soundtracks feel so physically punishing at home.

02:47.580 --> 02:51.920
We're moving way beyond that lazy industry excuse of, you know, it's too loud.

02:52.020 --> 02:53.520
Right, because that just doesn't cover it.

02:53.700 --> 02:54.260
It doesn't.

02:54.260 --> 03:02.400
So we are going to explore the fascinating and honestly kind of mind-blowing data science behind how we actually measure acoustic annoyance.

03:02.760 --> 03:04.240
It really is incredible stuff.

03:04.320 --> 03:10.040
Yeah, because if you're the person out there constantly riding the volume button on your couch, I promise you, you aren't crazy.

03:10.220 --> 03:11.080
No, you're really not.

03:11.300 --> 03:18.720
There is actual rigorous mathematical proof that these audio mixes are pushing you to the absolute brink.

03:18.860 --> 03:20.880
Yes, the math backs you up.

03:20.880 --> 03:28.500
So let's start at the beginning. When people complain about loud movies or TV shows, the industry does have a standard response, don't they?

03:28.720 --> 03:32.640
They do, yeah. And it is entirely based around overall averages.

03:32.760 --> 03:33.160
Averages.

03:33.380 --> 03:44.060
Right. When the volume of complaints about unintelligible dialogue mixed with deafening explosions reaches a certain pitch, the industry almost always points to standard loudness metrics.

03:44.280 --> 03:44.720
Like what?

03:44.720 --> 03:52.180
You'll hear audio engineers talk about things like LUFS, which stands for Loudness Units Relative to Full Scale.

03:52.280 --> 03:52.960
LUFS.

03:53.260 --> 03:53.580
Catchy.

03:53.680 --> 04:00.140
Yeah, it's a standardized algorithm, specifically one called ITU-RBS.1770.

04:00.140 --> 04:10.520
But without getting too bogged down in the math just yet, all you really need to know is that it measures the average perceived loudness of a piece of media over its entire runtime.

04:10.520 --> 04:18.460
Wait, so it's looking at the whole two-hour movie or the whole 30-minute sitcom and generating just one single score for how loud it was on average.

04:18.920 --> 04:20.540
Precisely. Just one number.

04:21.120 --> 04:23.420
And the industry leans heavily on this.

04:23.420 --> 04:29.380
I mean, in the broadcast television world, they have actually passed federal laws based on this exact algorithm.

04:29.600 --> 04:31.920
Oh, right. In the U.S., there was the Callum Act, right?

04:32.060 --> 04:34.800
Yes, the Commercial Advertisement Loudness Mitigation Act.

04:34.800 --> 04:36.920
Right, because I remember that law.

04:37.680 --> 04:44.240
It legally required television commercials to not exceed the average loudness of the program they accompanied.

04:44.240 --> 04:56.020
Exactly. The goal was to stop that jarring experience where, you know, a quiet golf tournament goes to commercial and suddenly a local used car salesman is screaming at you at twice the volume.

04:56.120 --> 05:01.620
I definitely remember that era of television. Nobody misses the screaming car commercials.

05:01.780 --> 05:03.560
No. Thankfully, that's mostly gone.

05:03.760 --> 05:12.380
But wait, if these laws exist and these strict algorithms are being used to measure loudness, why does my action movie still sound like it's trying to blast a hole through my drywall?

05:12.380 --> 05:17.280
No. I mean, measuring the average doesn't actually seem to solve the problem for feature films.

05:17.740 --> 05:19.520
It doesn't solve it at all.

05:20.360 --> 05:29.780
And Flyxion's paper does a phenomenal job of dismantling exactly why these industry measures fail so spectacularly when applied to movies.

05:29.940 --> 05:32.100
Okay, lay it on me. Why do they fail?

05:32.840 --> 05:41.380
The fundamental flaw with metrics like integrated loudness or even something called loudness range is that they are entirely distributional.

05:41.380 --> 05:43.300
Meaning what exactly?

05:43.300 --> 05:50.300
Meaning they give you a single average number or a statistical summary for an entire two-hour experience.

05:50.560 --> 05:52.140
Think about what that leaves out.

05:52.280 --> 05:53.200
A lot, I'd assume.

05:53.420 --> 05:55.760
A ton. It doesn't tell you when things are loud.

05:56.120 --> 05:58.880
And crucially, it doesn't tell you for how long they stay loud.

05:59.000 --> 06:00.900
Oh, I see. Okay, that makes a lot of sense.

06:00.900 --> 06:08.960
So it's like if I have a thermometer that tells me the average temperature of a room over a 24-hour period was, say, 70 degrees, that sounds perfectly comfortable.

06:09.100 --> 06:10.140
Right, a nice spring day.

06:10.280 --> 06:10.680
Exactly.

06:10.960 --> 06:18.760
But if the room was actually zero degrees for 12 hours and then 140 degrees for the other 12 hours, the average is completely useless.

06:18.960 --> 06:20.580
Because you still froze and then you boiled.

06:20.760 --> 06:21.040
Yes.

06:21.600 --> 06:24.160
The average hides the actual torture I went through.

06:24.160 --> 06:26.380
That is a fantastic way to frame it.

06:26.800 --> 06:31.900
The average hides the temporal reality, the actual experience over time.

06:32.000 --> 06:32.280
Right.

06:32.460 --> 06:35.980
Which leads us to the revolutionary concept introduced in this manuscript.

06:36.440 --> 06:38.220
The author calls it occupation.

06:38.720 --> 06:39.560
Occupation. Okay.

06:39.820 --> 06:45.820
The core argument here is that the physical discomfort you feel during a movie isn't just about peak intensity.

06:46.140 --> 06:52.500
It's not about the fact that one single explosion hit a super high decibel mark.

06:52.500 --> 06:53.520
Then what is it about?

06:54.140 --> 07:02.440
It is about how much time the soundtrack stays elevated well above the dialogue level without ever giving your auditory system a chance to recover.

07:02.840 --> 07:03.940
Acoustic occupation.

07:04.220 --> 07:04.480
Yeah.

07:04.960 --> 07:06.360
I love that word for this.

07:06.820 --> 07:11.100
It implies that your physical space has literally been taken over by a hostile force.

07:11.280 --> 07:12.160
It really has.

07:12.460 --> 07:14.360
So how does the paper define this scientifically?

07:14.800 --> 07:20.780
Well, Flyxion establishes three very strict conditions that must be met for true acoustic occupation to occur.

07:20.980 --> 07:22.020
Okay, what's condition one?

07:22.020 --> 07:26.440
Condition one, the listening volume in the room must be anchored to the dialogue.

07:26.740 --> 07:27.540
Anchored to the dialogue.

07:27.720 --> 07:27.840
Okay.

07:28.100 --> 07:37.460
Condition two, the non-dialogue sound, so the music, the sound effects, the explosions must wildly exceed that dialogue baseline.

07:37.620 --> 07:37.840
Right.

07:37.940 --> 07:39.660
The 140 degree heat wave part.

07:39.720 --> 07:40.200
Exactly.

07:40.200 --> 07:54.100
And condition three, which is arguably the most important, the sound must stay at that elevated level for long, continuous stretches of time, offering almost zero intervals for the listener's ears to drop back to a resting state.

07:54.100 --> 07:58.620
Okay, I want to stop and look at that first condition for a second because I think it's the root of everything else.

07:58.700 --> 07:59.560
The dialogue anchor.

07:59.560 --> 08:00.080
Yeah.

08:00.480 --> 08:04.800
Condition one, the listening volume is anchored to the dialogue.

08:05.520 --> 08:06.400
Why do we do that?

08:06.720 --> 08:12.460
Why do human beings automatically set their TV volume based on spoken words instead of the explosions?

08:12.740 --> 08:16.420
Because the dialogue reference is entirely internal to the film's narrative.

08:16.420 --> 08:19.060
Meaning, I have to hear it to know what's going on.

08:19.260 --> 08:19.660
Exactly.

08:20.060 --> 08:24.360
As a viewer, you must hear the words being spoken to understand the plot, right?

08:24.480 --> 08:24.600
Yeah.

08:24.680 --> 08:27.820
To track the character motivations and to actually engage with the story.

08:27.880 --> 08:29.600
Otherwise, I'm just watching a silent light show.

08:29.800 --> 08:29.980
Right.

08:30.440 --> 08:34.440
And human hearing is also evolutionarily primed to prioritize speech.

08:34.920 --> 08:37.320
We instinctively seek it out in noise.

08:37.640 --> 08:38.340
Oh, that makes sense.

08:38.340 --> 08:46.000
So, your volume baseline in your living room is completely at the mercy of how the audio mixer decided to treat that speech back in the studio.

08:46.000 --> 08:46.780
Oh, man.

08:47.340 --> 08:52.420
So, if the mixer buries the dialogue at a really low level relative to the background noise.

08:52.880 --> 08:58.800
You are forced to turn your television or receiver up just to comprehend what is happening on screen.

08:59.060 --> 09:04.960
So, by trying to just follow the story, I am essentially setting a trap for myself.

09:05.220 --> 09:05.700
You are.

09:05.700 --> 09:13.520
Like, the movie forces me to turn the volume up to a level of, say, 40 on my soundbar just to hear two people talking in a diner.

09:13.620 --> 09:13.840
Right.

09:13.840 --> 09:21.520
But by turning it up to 40, I have now primed my physical sound system to absolutely detonate when the action scene starts outside the diner.

09:21.600 --> 09:22.720
That is exactly the trap.

09:22.960 --> 09:25.740
You are a hostage to the film's internal logic.

09:26.380 --> 09:35.220
Going back to your temperature analogy earlier, using integrated loudness to measure a film is like pointing to the yearly average temperature of a city to prove the climate is perfectly fine.

09:35.360 --> 09:35.540
Right.

09:35.720 --> 09:36.840
Everything looks great on paper.

09:36.840 --> 09:44.080
Exactly, but it completely ignores the fact that the citizens just suffered through a 110 degree heat wave for three uninterrupted months.

09:44.080 --> 09:51.760
The average looks fine on a spreadsheet, but the occupation of the heat is what actually broke the power grid and exhausted the population.

09:51.760 --> 09:55.100
I am really starting to see how broken the industry standard is.

09:55.440 --> 10:04.680
Like, a film could alternate every 10 seconds between someone whispering and a massive gunshot back and forth for two hours.

10:04.780 --> 10:05.080
Yes.

10:05.260 --> 10:12.800
And another film could feature one solid hour of quiet whispering, followed by one uninterrupted hour of deafening machine gun fire.

10:12.800 --> 10:18.560
And under the LUFS algorithm, those two movies might have the exact same integrated loudness.

10:18.880 --> 10:21.880
But human biology does not experience them the same way at all.

10:21.980 --> 10:22.800
Not even close.

10:23.100 --> 10:29.060
The human auditory system experiences that second film as an aggressive, exhausting occupation of your senses.

10:29.460 --> 10:31.980
It makes me wonder what is driving this on the production side.

10:32.100 --> 10:34.380
Like, are film mixers just trying to torture us?

10:34.580 --> 10:36.180
It certainly feels like it sometimes.

10:36.340 --> 10:40.620
Right, because we saw this exact same thing happen in the music industry decades ago.

10:40.800 --> 10:42.700
The infamous loudness wars.

10:42.980 --> 10:43.540
Oh, yes.

10:44.280 --> 10:45.360
A dark time for audio.

10:45.960 --> 10:52.260
Every record label wanted their pop song to sound punchier and louder on the radio than the competitor's song.

10:52.620 --> 10:55.740
Right, so audio engineers just started compressing the music.

10:56.120 --> 10:58.800
Squashing the quiet parts up and the loud parts down.

10:58.920 --> 10:59.160
Yeah.

10:59.260 --> 11:03.740
Until the song was just a solid, unmoving brick of maximum sound.

11:03.920 --> 11:05.740
It was so exhausting to listen to.

11:05.780 --> 11:06.640
It really was.

11:06.640 --> 11:10.840
So, are film mixers feeling that same competitive pressure?

11:10.840 --> 11:20.360
Is there a Hollywood loudness war where directors feel they have to keep the audience pinned to the back of their seats to keep them from, I don't know, looking at their phones?

11:20.360 --> 11:22.700
The paper actually draws that exact parallel.

11:23.500 --> 11:27.780
It is highly probable that a similar arms race is happening in cinema right now.

11:27.860 --> 11:28.100
Really?

11:28.260 --> 11:28.480
Yeah.

11:28.880 --> 11:31.840
I mean, think about the technological leaps over the last 20 years.

11:31.920 --> 11:32.880
Like surround sound.

11:32.880 --> 11:33.440
Exactly.

11:33.700 --> 11:46.460
The introduction of digital multi-channel formats like 5.1 surround sound and later object-based audio like Dolby Atmos, that provided a massive expansion in dynamic range and low frequency capacity.

11:46.660 --> 11:48.560
So they just have way more power to play with.

11:48.820 --> 11:49.160
Yes.

11:49.160 --> 11:54.240
Suddenly, filmmakers had this gigantic canvas of sound they could paint on.

11:54.560 --> 12:01.580
They could make the lows lower and the louds immensely lighter without the physical limitations of old analog film strips.

12:01.660 --> 12:06.680
It's like handing a painter a canvas that is 50 feet tall instead of a standard easel.

12:07.020 --> 12:08.040
You don't want to fill the space.

12:08.380 --> 12:08.900
Precisely.

12:08.900 --> 12:17.240
But when you combine that massive technical headroom with the modern blockbuster's desire for intense, visceral action sequences...

12:17.240 --> 12:19.280
And the fear that audiences are getting bored.

12:19.480 --> 12:19.680
Right.

12:19.760 --> 12:25.760
When you pair that with a very real anxiety that audiences have shorter attention spans, you get a toxic mix.

12:25.880 --> 12:27.560
So they just turn everything up to 11.

12:27.820 --> 12:32.100
Directors and mixers can end up pushing towards sustained, high-level output.

12:32.540 --> 12:35.360
They use sound as a blunt instrument to command your attention.

12:35.360 --> 12:41.780
And over a two-hour runtime, that loss of dynamic contrast just completely wears out the home viewer.

12:42.020 --> 12:44.460
Okay, but this brings up a massive question for me.

12:44.820 --> 12:49.480
I am tracking with everything you're saying about occupation and the loss of dynamic contrast.

12:49.700 --> 12:49.980
Right.

12:50.280 --> 12:55.700
But if this mixing style is the disease, why do I only ever catch the symptoms in my own house?

12:55.980 --> 12:58.580
Ah, the location difference.

12:58.600 --> 12:58.780
Yeah.

12:59.000 --> 13:03.140
Because when I go to a commercial movie theater, I see the exact same blockbuster.

13:03.260 --> 13:04.000
Yes, you do.

13:04.000 --> 13:07.400
And the explosions are incredibly loud.

13:07.820 --> 13:13.280
In fact, they are probably physically louder in the theater than my TV could ever get at home.

13:13.380 --> 13:14.020
Almost certainly.

13:14.280 --> 13:17.320
But I never feel that urge to plug my ears in the theater.

13:17.820 --> 13:18.860
I never feel stressed.

13:19.020 --> 13:22.320
And crucially, I can almost always hear what the actors are whispering.

13:23.280 --> 13:28.400
Why is my living room such a hostile environment compared to the local multiplex?

13:28.400 --> 13:31.000
That is the pivotal question of this whole discussion.

13:31.200 --> 13:36.780
And the answer comes down to a fundamental mechanical difference in how audio is reproduced.

13:36.920 --> 13:37.840
Mechanical difference.

13:38.080 --> 13:38.420
Yes.

13:38.820 --> 13:42.900
To understand it, we have to look at how a theatrical mix is actually born.

13:43.540 --> 13:47.560
Theatrical reproduction is strictly, rigorously calibrated.

13:47.720 --> 13:48.280
Calibrated.

13:48.380 --> 13:48.780
How so?

13:48.780 --> 13:52.380
There is an international standard practice set by SMPTE.

13:52.680 --> 13:55.580
That's the Society of Motion Picture and Television Engineers.

13:55.800 --> 13:56.020
Okay.

13:56.420 --> 14:03.980
When a commercial theater is built, audio technicians come in with specialized pink noise generators and highly sensitive SPL meters.

14:04.240 --> 14:06.400
SPL meaning sound pressure level.

14:06.400 --> 14:07.360
Exactly.

14:07.740 --> 14:08.880
Sound pressure level.

14:09.480 --> 14:14.060
They place that meter in the exact center of the theater, which is the reference listening position.

14:14.600 --> 14:25.440
They then tune the amplifiers so that a specific digital reference level inside the audio file produces exactly 85 decibels of acoustic sound pressure in that room.

14:25.620 --> 14:25.820
Okay.

14:26.180 --> 14:27.920
I want to make sure I'm visualizing this correctly.

14:28.360 --> 14:31.960
So an audio file is basically just data on a hard drive, right?

14:32.000 --> 14:32.160
Right.

14:32.200 --> 14:32.780
It's just numbers.

14:32.780 --> 14:36.140
It has numbers ranging from zero down to negative infinity.

14:36.400 --> 14:45.240
But you're saying the theater is physically tuned so that when the hard drive spits out a specific number, the physical speaker cone moves a specific amount.

14:45.460 --> 14:45.640
Yes.

14:46.000 --> 14:50.640
And hits the audience member's ear with exactly 85 decibels of physical force.

14:50.740 --> 14:52.820
That is precisely what calibration means.

14:53.260 --> 14:58.140
It is a locked mathematical relationship between the digital file and the physical room.

14:58.260 --> 14:58.620
Wow.

14:58.820 --> 15:00.940
Now here is why that matters for your experience.

15:00.940 --> 15:12.700
When the director and the sound mixer are sitting in their million-dollar mixing stage in Los Angeles, authoring the movie, their room is calibrated to that exact same SMPTE 85 decibel standard.

15:12.780 --> 15:13.080
Ah.

15:13.620 --> 15:14.300
So they aren't guessing.

15:14.540 --> 15:15.520
They are never guessing.

15:15.860 --> 15:23.100
They know with absolute certainty how loud the dialogue will be in a theater in York, Tokyo, or London.

15:23.240 --> 15:25.060
Because every theater is locked to the same map.

15:25.060 --> 15:25.580
Exactly.

15:26.000 --> 15:35.920
Because of that locked standard, they can safely mix the dialogue at a comfortable level, and they can surgically choose exactly how much louder the explosion should be relative to that speech.

15:36.340 --> 15:43.500
The spatial relationships, the frequency balances, the dynamics, they are all authored for a known acoustic environment.

15:43.900 --> 15:45.780
And the living room has literally none of that.

15:45.940 --> 15:46.500
None at all.

15:46.500 --> 15:49.820
Like, my TV has a volume slider from 1 to 100.

15:50.400 --> 15:54.040
My sound bar has a little dial with some white LED dots on it.

15:54.420 --> 15:57.860
Neither of those readouts correlate to actual decibels in the room.

15:58.180 --> 15:58.620
Exactly.

15:58.880 --> 16:01.200
Home reproduction is completely uncalibrated.

16:01.360 --> 16:02.500
It is the wild west.

16:02.700 --> 16:04.060
The wild west of audio.

16:04.280 --> 16:06.180
The viewer is just manually guessing.

16:06.400 --> 16:10.300
You're turning the dial up until your brain says, okay, I can hear the speech now.

16:10.440 --> 16:15.800
And the moment you do that, you destroy the carefully calibrated relationship the filmmaker authored.

16:15.800 --> 16:18.820
So the moment I touch the dial, I'm ruining the mix.

16:19.240 --> 16:19.540
Yes.

16:20.260 --> 16:23.300
The consequences of this can be catastrophic for the audio.

16:23.840 --> 16:35.920
In fact, Flyxion centers a large portion of this manuscript around a very specific, and I will admit, somewhat extreme case study of a home reproduction chain to prove exactly how badly things can break down.

16:36.020 --> 16:36.580
Oh, man.

16:37.000 --> 16:41.120
Let's get into this case study, because when I read this part of the outline, I had a laugh.

16:41.320 --> 16:42.240
It is quite the setup.

16:42.240 --> 16:49.200
Paint a picture of the exact hardware setup this listener was using to watch a major Hollywood action film.

16:49.680 --> 16:56.020
So the author analyzed a listener who was watching the film from a standard desktop computer.

16:56.180 --> 16:56.460
Okay.

16:56.780 --> 16:57.640
Pretty normal so far.

16:57.720 --> 16:57.880
Right.

16:58.180 --> 17:01.340
The audio was coming out of the computer's front panel headphone jack.

17:01.340 --> 17:06.540
It was traveling through a standard, cheap, 3.5mm auxiliary cable.

17:06.760 --> 17:06.980
Okay.

17:07.120 --> 17:12.420
And plugged directly into a small, solid-state bass guitar practice amplifier.

17:12.580 --> 17:13.100
Wait, what?

17:13.600 --> 17:19.140
Specifically, an Orange Crush 25BX, which features a single 8-inch speaker driver.

17:19.200 --> 17:19.880
Okay, hold on.

17:20.180 --> 17:21.340
A bass guitar amp.

17:21.520 --> 17:23.060
That is genuinely wild.

17:23.060 --> 17:28.620
I know we're doing serious academic analysis here, but part of me wants to reject this entirely.

17:29.460 --> 17:38.120
A bass amp is designed to play a four-string guitar in a garage, not to reproduce the delicate 5.1 surround mix of a Hollywood blockbuster.

17:38.200 --> 17:39.140
I know, I know.

17:39.260 --> 17:41.600
Isn't this listener just an absurd outlier?

17:42.100 --> 17:49.280
It feels like testing a sports car's performance by putting square wheels on it and acting surprised when it doesn't drive well.

17:49.280 --> 17:57.840
It is an extreme setup, without a doubt, but if we connect this to the bigger picture, from a research perspective, it is actually the perfect stress test.

17:58.100 --> 18:01.840
Why? How is a bass amp a good test for cinema sound?

18:02.280 --> 18:09.840
Because it represents the ultimate distillation of the cobbled-together, totally uncalibrated setups that millions of people actually use.

18:10.020 --> 18:11.680
Okay, I guess people do use weird setups.

18:11.680 --> 18:19.800
Think about college dorm rooms using cheap Bluetooth boomboxes, or budget televisions with rear-firing speakers jammed against a plaster wall.

18:20.180 --> 18:26.800
The bass amp is just an exaggerated version of the very real mechanical failures present in almost all home audio.

18:27.020 --> 18:28.960
So it's magnifying the flaws we all have.

18:29.260 --> 18:29.800
Exactly.

18:30.420 --> 18:37.020
Fakroi uses the specific setup to break down how the physical hardware fundamentally distorts the filmmaker's intent.

18:37.020 --> 18:44.420
They identify four distinct compounding transformations that ruin the sound before it ever reaches the ear.

18:44.940 --> 18:47.520
Okay, I will accept the bass amp as a stress test.

18:48.040 --> 18:49.840
Let's walk through these four transformations.

18:50.400 --> 18:52.640
How does this setup destroy the movie?

18:53.200 --> 18:55.440
The first transformation is channel reduction.

18:55.660 --> 18:56.280
Channel reduction.

18:56.580 --> 18:59.740
The film was originally authored in 5.1 surround sound.

19:00.100 --> 19:02.880
That means there are six discrete audio channels.

19:02.880 --> 19:08.260
You have left, center, right, two rears, and a subwoofer track.

19:08.900 --> 19:13.280
In a proper theater mix, spatial separation is used as a critical tool for clarity.

19:13.480 --> 19:15.200
Spatial separation, meaning where the sound comes from.

19:15.280 --> 19:15.580
Yes.

19:16.200 --> 19:19.800
The dialogue is anchored strictly to the center channel speaker behind the screen.

19:20.200 --> 19:24.440
The music and the chaotic explosions are pushed out to the left, right, and surround speakers.

19:24.860 --> 19:29.720
The physical space between the speakers allows your brain to easily separate the speech from the noise.

19:30.180 --> 19:32.020
But the bass amp only has one speaker.

19:32.020 --> 19:32.660
Exactly.

19:33.380 --> 19:38.220
When you play a 5.1 mix out of a stereo headphone jack into a mono bass amp,

19:38.660 --> 19:43.040
the computer's media player has to perform a massive mathematical compromise.

19:43.300 --> 19:44.660
It has to blend them all together.

19:44.880 --> 19:49.660
It squashes all six of those separate channels down into a single mono track.

19:50.000 --> 19:53.120
All that spatial separation instantly vanishes.

19:53.460 --> 19:53.980
Oh, wow.

19:53.980 --> 20:01.680
The whispered dialogue and the roaring explosions are suddenly being forced out of the exact same 8-inch paper cone simultaneously.

20:02.040 --> 20:05.760
They are literally fighting for the exact same acoustic real estate.

20:06.140 --> 20:10.320
So without the physical space in the ream, the sounds just step all over each other.

20:10.440 --> 20:10.720
Yes.

20:10.800 --> 20:15.040
It's like trying to listen to someone talk to you while a marching band is standing an inch behind them.

20:15.060 --> 20:17.040
It's impossible for your brain to sort out.

20:17.040 --> 20:18.560
Okay, what is the second transformation?

20:18.560 --> 20:23.580
The second is band limitation, and this one directly attacks the human voice.

20:23.840 --> 20:24.800
Attacking the voice.

20:24.960 --> 20:25.140
How?

20:25.300 --> 20:29.500
Well, there is a metric in audiology called the speech intelligibility index.

20:29.500 --> 20:35.120
It maps out which frequencies of sound are most important for the human brain to comprehend words.

20:35.380 --> 20:41.300
And it turns out we rely overwhelmingly on the frequency band between 1 and 4 kilohertz.

20:41.580 --> 20:45.520
Okay, so what kinds of sounds live in that 1 to 4 kilohertz range?

20:45.640 --> 20:46.180
Consonants.

20:46.560 --> 20:49.160
The sharp, crisp sounds that give language its structure.

20:49.680 --> 20:51.740
The Ts, the Ks, the Ss.

20:51.920 --> 20:52.040
Oh.

20:52.500 --> 20:55.820
Vowels carry the volume of speech, but consonants carry the meaning.

20:56.460 --> 20:59.800
Without consonants, speech just sounds like muffled humming.

21:00.100 --> 21:01.320
Right, like Charlie Brown's teacher.

21:01.380 --> 21:01.740
Exactly.

21:02.100 --> 21:03.080
Now think about the hardware.

21:03.660 --> 21:06.960
This listener is using an Orange Crush bass amplifier.

21:07.200 --> 21:13.180
That cabinet and that speaker are specifically voiced by the manufacturer to reproduce low frequency energy,

21:13.660 --> 21:15.720
the deep thumps of a bass guitar.

21:15.840 --> 21:17.520
Oh no, I see where this is going.

21:17.680 --> 21:22.480
By its very spherical design, it inherently suppresses and rolls off the higher frequencies,

21:22.480 --> 21:26.200
specifically that vital 1 to 4 kilohertz range.

21:26.420 --> 21:26.740
Oh wow.

21:27.020 --> 21:29.900
So transformation 1 removed the physical space around the dialogue,

21:30.200 --> 21:35.220
and now transformation 2 is actively muffling the exact frequencies needed to tell words apart.

21:36.060 --> 21:39.240
It's essentially acting as a consonant eraser.

21:39.920 --> 21:40.480
That is brutal.

21:41.220 --> 21:42.000
And it gets worse.

21:42.120 --> 21:43.000
How could it get worse?

21:43.860 --> 21:46.300
The third transformation introduces active harm.

21:46.740 --> 21:48.800
It's called level-dependent distortion.

21:49.300 --> 21:50.500
Level-dependent distortion.

21:50.640 --> 21:51.660
Okay, break that down for me.

21:51.660 --> 21:55.400
Think about the input jack on a bass amplifier.

21:56.360 --> 22:03.820
It is engineered to receive a relatively weak, low-voltage signal from the passive magnetic pickups of a guitar.

22:04.160 --> 22:06.920
Right, a guitar isn't outputting a lot of electricity.

22:07.080 --> 22:07.580
Exactly.

22:08.240 --> 22:11.560
But the headphone jack of a computer, when turned up,

22:11.920 --> 22:14.880
outputs a significantly hotter line-level signal.

22:14.880 --> 22:20.220
So you're feeding the amp way more electrical power than it was ever designed to handle at the input stage.

22:20.220 --> 22:20.660
Yes.

22:21.280 --> 22:25.520
During the quiet dialogue scenes, the signal is weak enough that the amp handles it okay.

22:26.140 --> 22:29.620
But the moment an action sequence hits, the audio level spikes.

22:30.140 --> 22:35.500
That hot signal slams into the amplifier's input circuitry and exceeds its clean operating range.

22:35.500 --> 22:37.060
Much of the circuitry just overloads.

22:37.060 --> 22:39.920
The technical term is clipping the preamplifier.

22:40.280 --> 22:44.720
The top and bottom of the audio waveform literally get chopped off by the circuitry.

22:45.360 --> 22:51.220
And when a waveform clips, it generates harsh, unnatural harmonic distortion.

22:51.480 --> 22:51.800
Wait, wait.

22:52.220 --> 22:53.280
Let me make sure I'm getting this.

22:53.280 --> 22:55.880
It's not just that the loud parts are loud.

22:55.880 --> 23:04.340
But you are saying the hardware itself is actually generating new, abrasive, distorted noise that was never even in the original audio file.

23:04.460 --> 23:04.860
Precisely.

23:04.960 --> 23:10.320
The amplifier is panicking, basically, and adding harsh harmonic fuzz to the explosions.

23:10.680 --> 23:13.160
The hardware is actively generating pain.

23:13.220 --> 23:14.100
That is insane.

23:14.320 --> 23:19.540
Which leads perfectly into the fourth and final transformation, the coupling of the controls.

23:19.640 --> 23:20.380
Okay, the controls.

23:20.380 --> 23:27.180
Because the listener is sitting at a desk, their primary volume control is the digital slider on their computer monitor,

23:27.560 --> 23:30.720
which is located before the signal reaches the amplifier.

23:30.900 --> 23:32.120
Oh, I see where this is going.

23:32.500 --> 23:37.120
By turning the computer volume down during the explosions, they aren't just lowering the decibels.

23:37.280 --> 23:46.080
They are lowering the voltage hitting the amp, which means turning the volume down reduces the raw noise and relieves the physical distortion simultaneously.

23:46.320 --> 23:47.040
Double the relief.

23:47.040 --> 23:47.600
Yes.

23:48.160 --> 23:54.980
They have an immense compounding incentive to turn it down during the action because they aren't just making it quieter.

23:55.160 --> 23:58.900
They are stopping the physical hardware from tearing the audio apart.

23:59.060 --> 24:02.300
This completely upends how I thought about TV audio.

24:02.420 --> 24:02.540
Yeah.

24:02.620 --> 24:05.660
Like, I always assumed bad sound was just a file issue.

24:05.820 --> 24:06.720
A lot of people do.

24:06.720 --> 24:18.160
But we have a scenario here where the channel mixdown ruins the space, the speaker cone ruins the consonants, and the voltage mismatch generates literal distortion.

24:18.400 --> 24:20.700
It's a perfect storm of acoustic failure.

24:21.200 --> 24:27.700
There's a theory mentioned in the paper, I think, from a French theorist named Michel Kion about cinema being vococentric.

24:27.700 --> 24:28.260
Yes.

24:28.500 --> 24:31.300
Michel Kion is a foundational film sound theorist.

24:31.720 --> 24:41.200
He argues that cinema sound is entirely hokocentric, meaning the entire acoustic world of the film is organized around and subservient to the human voice.

24:41.360 --> 24:43.080
So the voice is the most important thing.

24:43.220 --> 24:46.700
The voice is the sun, and the sound effects are the planets orbiting it.

24:46.700 --> 24:57.240
Flyxion argues that this bass amp setup, and by extension many cheap living room sound bars, represents a catastrophic failure of vococentrism at the point of reproduction.

24:57.800 --> 25:00.600
The hardware has actively rebelled against the voice.

25:00.940 --> 25:06.780
Okay, so we thoroughly understand the mechanical reality of why the living room hardware is sabotaging us.

25:06.800 --> 25:07.140
Right.

25:07.140 --> 25:14.360
But if every living room is different, I mean, some have bass amps, some have sound bars, some have tiny flat screen TV speakers.

25:14.360 --> 25:17.860
How do you even begin to study this scientifically?

25:18.320 --> 25:19.300
That is the big challenge.

25:19.320 --> 25:23.260
Like, because you can't just go to a thousand different houses, sit on people's couches, and take notes.

25:23.660 --> 25:28.760
To prove this occupation is happening, you have to look at the source audio itself mathematically.

25:29.300 --> 25:31.660
That is the exact hurdle the researcher faced.

25:31.860 --> 25:34.900
You need objective data from the audio file itself.

25:35.400 --> 25:41.900
And to get that, Flyxion had to build a custom data analysis pipeline using the Python programming language.

25:41.900 --> 25:45.660
Okay, I am not a programmer, so you're going to have to walk me through how you feed a movie into code.

25:45.980 --> 25:48.360
It is a massive computational challenge.

25:48.760 --> 25:54.720
They used a command line tool called FFmpeg to decode the raw audio from the movie file.

25:54.940 --> 25:56.260
Okay, FFmpeg.

25:56.340 --> 26:01.760
And a specialized Python library called Librosa to analyze the acoustic features.

26:02.840 --> 26:05.600
Librosa is standard in music information retrieval.

26:05.600 --> 26:10.980
It can look at a sound wave and extract mathematical data about its pitch, its rhythm, its tone.

26:11.180 --> 26:11.980
That sounds handy.

26:12.160 --> 26:12.660
It is.

26:13.140 --> 26:18.740
But analyzing a three-minute pop song is very different from analyzing a two-hour blockbuster movie.

26:18.840 --> 26:20.960
Yeah, I'd imagine the file size is way bigger.

26:21.300 --> 26:21.780
Massive.

26:22.140 --> 26:27.900
A standard film mix with six channels of audio playing at a sampling rate of 48,000 times a second

26:27.900 --> 26:32.380
results in roughly 1.5 billion individual audio samples.

26:32.440 --> 26:34.120
1.5 billion points of data.

26:34.120 --> 26:37.100
You can't just load that into the RAM of a standard laptop.

26:37.560 --> 26:39.240
I assume it would just freeze instantly.

26:39.400 --> 26:39.860
It did.

26:40.280 --> 26:44.640
The author notes that their first attempt to process the whole film simultaneously completely

26:44.640 --> 26:46.740
crashed their operating system out of memory.

26:46.880 --> 26:47.440
I'm not surprised.

26:47.660 --> 26:53.420
Yeah, they had to rewrite the entire pipeline to stream the audio in small 60-second chunks,

26:53.980 --> 26:58.880
extracting only the necessary mathematical features ran by frame, and then throwing the raw

26:58.880 --> 27:00.480
audio away just to save memory.

27:00.620 --> 27:02.540
That is some serious dedication to the data.

27:02.540 --> 27:09.280
But what I absolutely loved about reading this manuscript was that Flyxion deliberately chose

27:09.280 --> 27:13.320
to include their failed early detection instruments in the final text.

27:13.440 --> 27:15.800
Yes, that was a very transparent choice.

27:15.960 --> 27:16.180
Right.

27:16.280 --> 27:18.360
They didn't just show the polished final results.

27:18.640 --> 27:20.340
They showed the messy trial and error.

27:20.880 --> 27:23.460
Why is the swell detector such a great example of this?

27:23.560 --> 27:27.640
Oh, the swell detector failure is a perfect lesson in how math can blind us.

27:27.780 --> 27:28.320
How so?

27:28.320 --> 27:33.760
Well, Flyxion wanted to build an algorithmic instrument that could automatically detect slow,

27:34.020 --> 27:35.500
rising swells in the audio.

27:36.180 --> 27:41.980
Think of a long, ominous buildup of musical tension, or a low rumble that gradually gets

27:41.980 --> 27:42.340
louder.

27:42.540 --> 27:42.760
Okay.

27:42.980 --> 27:43.720
I can picture that.

27:44.100 --> 27:48.880
To do this, the algorithm smoothed out the volume data and was programmed to only look at

27:48.880 --> 27:49.880
the positive gradient.

27:50.060 --> 27:50.680
Meaning what?

27:50.680 --> 27:56.480
Meaning it completely ignored moments when the volume was dropping and only logged data

27:56.480 --> 27:58.360
when the volume was actively going up.

27:58.640 --> 27:59.760
That makes logical sense.

27:59.860 --> 28:00.000
Yeah.

28:00.200 --> 28:03.020
If you want to find a swell, you only care about sound getting louder.

28:03.180 --> 28:03.440
Right.

28:03.660 --> 28:04.780
It seems perfectly logical.

28:05.240 --> 28:10.620
But then to make the data usable, they tried to standardize it using a common statistical

28:10.620 --> 28:14.980
tool called the median absolute deviation, or MAD.

28:15.420 --> 28:15.700
Okay.

28:15.800 --> 28:16.480
Stop right there.

28:16.840 --> 28:18.300
Median absolute deviation.

28:18.300 --> 28:23.100
I took statistics in college, but you need to refresh my memory on how that works and

28:23.100 --> 28:24.220
why it broke the algorithm.

28:24.360 --> 28:25.400
Let's do a simple example.

28:25.720 --> 28:27.040
Imagine a sequence of numbers.

28:27.320 --> 28:29.480
Two, four, six, eight, ten.

28:29.660 --> 28:29.880
Okay.

28:30.160 --> 28:31.960
The middle number is six.

28:32.460 --> 28:36.020
The deviation is how far away the other numbers are from that middle point.

28:36.320 --> 28:38.360
It's a way to measure how spread out your data is.

28:38.440 --> 28:38.700
Okay.

28:38.820 --> 28:39.140
Got it.

28:39.340 --> 28:41.380
But remember what we just said about the movie audio?

28:42.080 --> 28:46.320
At any given microsecond, the volume is just as likely to be falling as it is rising.

28:46.620 --> 28:46.920
Oh.

28:47.360 --> 28:48.000
Oh, I see.

28:48.000 --> 28:52.540
Because the algorithm clipped all falling volume to zero, more than half of the billions

28:52.540 --> 28:54.420
of data points were exactly zero.

28:54.520 --> 28:54.820
Ah.

28:55.240 --> 28:58.860
If I have a list of numbers that is mostly zeros, the median number is zero.

28:59.360 --> 28:59.840
Exactly.

29:00.120 --> 29:01.580
The median is zero.

29:02.000 --> 29:06.300
And because most of the numbers are zero, the deviation from zero is also zero.

29:06.300 --> 29:10.160
So the median absolute deviation evaluated to exactly zero.

29:10.420 --> 29:14.640
And as anyone who has ever used a calculator knows, you cannot divide a number by zero.

29:15.040 --> 29:16.200
The universe implodes.

29:16.200 --> 29:19.080
The scaling function completely broke.

29:19.080 --> 29:25.180
The code defaulted to a backup formula that, mathematically speaking, essentially just produced

29:25.180 --> 29:27.720
an inverted copy of the acoustic transience.

29:27.720 --> 29:30.480
The sharp, sudden spikes in sound.

29:30.600 --> 29:33.220
So it did the exact opposite of what it was supposed to do.

29:33.260 --> 29:33.600
Yes.

29:33.940 --> 29:34.140
Yeah.

29:34.140 --> 29:40.480
So instead of finding slow, gradual swells, the algorithm confidently pointed at the sharpest,

29:40.580 --> 29:47.420
most sudden impacts in the movie, like a single gunshot or a door slamming, and labeled them as swells.

29:47.420 --> 29:47.900
That hurts.

29:47.900 --> 29:49.760
The author called it confident nonsense.

29:50.440 --> 29:56.680
What's fascinating here is that the math executed perfectly, but it represented a completely absurd human reality.

29:56.680 --> 29:58.700
I love that phrase, confident nonsense.

29:58.880 --> 30:01.740
It really highlights the danger of just trusting code.

30:02.320 --> 30:04.600
But the second failure was even wilder to me.

30:04.700 --> 30:05.580
The harshness score.

30:05.920 --> 30:06.240
Yes.

30:06.800 --> 30:13.000
They tried to build a harshness score to measure how abrasive the audio was to the human ear.

30:13.140 --> 30:13.400
Yes.

30:13.720 --> 30:18.080
They instructed Labrosa to combine several complex audio metrics.

30:18.080 --> 30:24.320
One was the spectral centroid, which essentially measures where the center of mass is in a sound.

30:24.680 --> 30:27.560
So if it's a high piercing sound, the centroid is high.

30:27.760 --> 30:28.180
Exactly.

30:28.520 --> 30:30.540
They also use the zero crossing rate.

30:30.940 --> 30:31.580
Let me guess.

30:31.720 --> 30:33.740
I need a translation for zero crossing rate too.

30:34.060 --> 30:35.120
It's actually pretty simple.

30:35.380 --> 30:41.700
Picture a sound wave on a graph fluctuating up and down across a horizontal center line, the zero line.

30:41.860 --> 30:42.160
Okay.

30:42.160 --> 30:48.240
The zero crossing rate is literally just counting how many times the wave crosses that center line in a given second.

30:48.400 --> 30:49.880
So what does that tell us about the sound?

30:50.260 --> 30:54.020
Well, a smooth, low bass note crosses very slowly.

30:54.440 --> 30:59.560
A jagged, harsh hissing noise crosses it thousands and thousands of times very rapidly.

30:59.860 --> 31:04.420
So a high zero crossing rate usually equals a harsh, noisy sound.

31:04.500 --> 31:04.720
Okay.

31:04.960 --> 31:10.960
So they combine these metrics, feed the action movie into the harshness detector, and what did the algorithm spit out?

31:10.960 --> 31:21.500
The algorithm flagged the absolute harshest, most abrasive, most punishing moment of the entire two-hour movie as occurring exactly at the 81-minute mark.

31:21.680 --> 31:25.760
The data showed a massive, unprecedented spike in harshness.

31:25.980 --> 31:33.840
But when the researcher actually pulled up the video file and listened to the movie at 81 minutes, what was happening on screen?

31:33.960 --> 31:34.700
Absolutely nothing.

31:34.800 --> 31:35.940
It was a scene transition.

31:36.320 --> 31:38.680
Total, absolute digital silence.

31:38.780 --> 31:39.200
Wait, what?

31:39.200 --> 31:42.360
The audio file dropped to negative 100 decilals.

31:42.580 --> 31:42.820
Wait.

31:43.780 --> 31:55.200
The algorithm analyzed the entire action movie, full of explosions and screaming, and confidently declared that the single harshest, most abrasive sound of the human ear was literal silence.

31:55.840 --> 31:56.300
Yes.

31:56.580 --> 31:58.400
How was that even computationally possible?

31:58.640 --> 32:02.060
This is perhaps the most important warning in the paper about data science.

32:02.380 --> 32:04.700
The danger of level-invariant features.

32:04.860 --> 32:05.680
Level-invariant.

32:05.940 --> 32:07.480
Meaning it doesn't care about volume.

32:07.480 --> 32:08.080
Exactly.

32:08.300 --> 32:12.140
Remember, metrics like the zero crossing rate measure the shape of a sound wave.

32:12.480 --> 32:14.120
They do not care how loud the wave is.

32:14.440 --> 32:17.800
When an audio file drops to digital silence, it isn't truly empty.

32:18.400 --> 32:26.320
No, there is a microscopic, mathematically generated noise floor at the very bottom of the file, often called dither.

32:26.680 --> 32:30.640
It is added to prevent digital quantization errors.

32:30.640 --> 32:34.760
So there is a tiny, invisible hiss of static at the bottom of the map.

32:34.860 --> 32:35.160
Yes.

32:35.480 --> 32:40.040
And that microscopic dither noise is spectrally perfectly flat.

32:40.520 --> 32:42.660
It is full of high-frequency energy.

32:43.080 --> 32:45.500
And its wave is incredibly jagged.

32:45.640 --> 32:46.400
Oh my gosh.

32:46.400 --> 32:55.500
So the algorithm zoomed in on that microscopic silence, looked at the jagged shape of the dither, and said, wow, the zero crossing rate here is off the charts.

32:55.620 --> 32:57.580
This is incredibly harsh and noisy.

32:57.680 --> 32:59.020
It completely ignored the volume.

32:59.220 --> 33:07.380
The algorithm was completely blind to the fact that this harsh noise was operating at negative 100 decibels, far below the threshold of human hearing.

33:07.380 --> 33:14.500
That is such a fascinating trap, and it points to a massive vulnerability we have when we use AI or machine learning.

33:14.740 --> 33:15.480
Confirmation bias.

33:15.860 --> 33:16.260
Exactly.

33:17.060 --> 33:17.760
Confirmation bias.

33:17.860 --> 33:18.280
Think about it.

33:18.560 --> 33:26.580
If you build an AI tool to find the most intense moments in a movie, and the AI spits out a timestamp, and you go look at it, and it's a massive car crash.

33:26.680 --> 33:29.660
You're going to nod your head and say, wow, the AI is a genius.

33:30.100 --> 33:34.600
You validate the algorithm because the result matches your expectation.

33:34.600 --> 33:47.300
But here, the AI pointed to total silence, which violently broke the expectation and forced the researcher to realize the underlying math was fundamentally detached from human biology.

33:47.560 --> 33:47.860
Exactly.

33:48.060 --> 33:50.180
An algorithm does not have a human ear.

33:50.440 --> 33:52.200
It doesn't know what pain feels like.

33:52.680 --> 33:53.820
So how do they fix it?

33:54.160 --> 34:01.320
To fix these broken instruments, Flyxion had to introduce something called audibility weighting into the Python code.

34:01.540 --> 34:02.520
Audibility weighting.

34:02.520 --> 34:06.160
They essentially had to give the algorithm a virtual pair of ears.

34:06.500 --> 34:07.920
They added a strict rule.

34:08.380 --> 34:15.640
If the overall volume of a sound is below negative 65 decibels, multiply all the harshness and transient scores by zero.

34:15.980 --> 34:18.020
Mathematically force the algorithm to ignore it.

34:18.060 --> 34:18.300
Yes.

34:18.600 --> 34:20.800
If a human ear can't hear it, it does not count.

34:20.940 --> 34:22.580
So they finally calibrated the instruments.

34:22.760 --> 34:23.800
The AI has ears.

34:23.940 --> 34:25.680
The math is grounded in human biology.

34:26.180 --> 34:29.120
It's time to put the actual movie on the operating table.

34:29.220 --> 34:30.160
Yes, the case study.

34:30.160 --> 34:34.680
The case study subject for this paper is a 2026 action thriller called Drive Thru Fire.

34:35.720 --> 34:39.660
What did the human listener actually report feeling when they watched this specific movie?

34:40.220 --> 34:44.040
The qualitative report from the listener was very specific and very frustrated.

34:44.720 --> 34:49.980
They noted that the dialogue was mixed comically low compared to the background drums and the music.

34:50.100 --> 34:51.060
A classic complaint.

34:51.060 --> 35:00.780
They highlighted extremely long, exhausting sequences of automatic gunfire and hand-to-hand combat and the most critical piece of data.

35:00.940 --> 35:01.320
What was it?

35:01.560 --> 35:14.620
They reported that during the final climax of the film, the action sequences became so overwhelming that they had to manually turn the volume fully off complete mute for stretches of one to two entire minutes at a time.

35:14.740 --> 35:15.040
Wow.

35:15.040 --> 35:18.340
Just sitting in silence, waiting for the visual chaos to end.

35:18.740 --> 35:22.700
Having to mute a movie for two straight minutes just to endure it sounds miserable.

35:22.940 --> 35:25.000
It defeats the entire purpose of the media.

35:25.100 --> 35:25.840
It really does.

35:26.180 --> 35:32.900
So, when Flyxion ran Drive Thru Fire through the newly corrected audibility-weighted data pipeline,

35:33.360 --> 35:35.600
did the math validate the listener's misery?

35:35.720 --> 35:37.720
It did, and the numbers are staggering.

35:37.780 --> 35:38.320
Let's hear them.

35:38.660 --> 35:42.880
The pipeline analyzed the film specifically looking for recovery windows.

35:43.280 --> 35:44.680
What counts as a recovery window?

35:44.680 --> 35:53.580
The paper defines a recovery window as an interval of at least five seconds, where the volume successfully drops back down to the quiet, baseline dialogue level.

35:53.640 --> 35:54.720
Okay, a five-second break.

35:55.120 --> 36:01.660
For the majority of the film's runtime, the median gap between these recovery windows is about 17.2 seconds.

36:01.800 --> 36:02.060
Okay.

36:02.280 --> 36:10.020
Meaning, for the first hour or so, the film gets loud, but roughly every 17 seconds, it gives your ears a five-second break.

36:10.320 --> 36:12.480
It breathes in, and it breathes out.

36:12.480 --> 36:17.480
17 seconds between breaks seems totally reasonable for an action movie.

36:17.580 --> 36:18.760
You expect it to be loud.

36:19.140 --> 36:21.440
But that rhythm didn't hold for the whole movie, did it?

36:21.520 --> 36:22.780
No, it completely collapses.

36:22.780 --> 36:32.980
The data revealed that during the massive extended climax of the film, there is a stretch where the longest gap without any relative recovery is 694.7 seconds.

36:32.980 --> 36:36.040
That is over 11 and a half minutes.

36:36.040 --> 36:40.040
Nearly 12 minutes where the audio never once returns to the dialogue baseline.

36:40.620 --> 36:43.660
The data traces for volume, for transience, for harshness.

36:44.000 --> 36:46.760
They just stay agonizingly high, pinned to the ceiling.

36:47.120 --> 36:52.940
Imagine trying to hold a heavy weight above your head for 12 straight minutes without lowering your arms once.

36:52.940 --> 36:58.480
That is essentially what this film's mix is asking your auditory system to do.

36:58.540 --> 37:00.100
It's physical exhaustion.

37:00.100 --> 37:03.180
But it isn't just about the sheer volume staying high, right?

37:03.700 --> 37:10.380
There is a qualitative textural difference to the sound during that climax that makes it so unbearable.

37:10.600 --> 37:10.960
Yes.

37:11.400 --> 37:17.100
To explain why those 12 minutes feel so awful, the paper introduces a concept called overdetermination.

37:17.940 --> 37:19.100
Overdetermination.

37:19.100 --> 37:25.600
And to grasp overdetermination, we first have to look at the theory of density proposed by Walter Murch.

37:25.720 --> 37:27.520
Oh, Walter Murch is an absolute legend.

37:27.700 --> 37:30.500
He edited Apocalypse Now, The Godfather Part 2.

37:30.920 --> 37:33.420
The man basically invented the term sound designer.

37:34.100 --> 37:35.360
What is his theory of density?

37:35.720 --> 37:45.360
Murch theorized that human cognition has a hard limit on how many simultaneous, distinct layers of sound an audience can track and derive meaning from at any one time.

37:45.420 --> 37:47.180
Like how many things we can listen to at once.

37:47.520 --> 37:48.000
Exactly.

37:48.000 --> 37:51.980
He suggested the brain can handle maybe two or three distinct layers.

37:52.640 --> 37:58.240
If you have a character speaking, the sound of footsteps on gravel, and a distant radio playing, the brain can separate them.

37:58.400 --> 37:59.540
It paints a 3D picture.

37:59.840 --> 37:59.980
Right.

38:00.180 --> 38:04.840
But if you add too many layers of the same type of sound, the brain gives up.

38:05.040 --> 38:07.800
The layers collapse into an undifferentiated mass.

38:08.100 --> 38:11.240
It loses all narrative meaning and just becomes noise.

38:11.440 --> 38:11.600
Right.

38:11.680 --> 38:13.340
It just turns to acoustic mud.

38:13.760 --> 38:16.980
So how does Flyxion build on Murch to define overdetermination?

38:16.980 --> 38:24.860
The Python pipeline tracks four different salience channels, which are basically four different ways a soundtrack can demand a viewer's attention.

38:25.220 --> 38:26.100
What are the four channels?

38:26.580 --> 38:29.340
One is transients, those sharp impacts like punches.

38:29.840 --> 38:34.280
Two is low frequency hits like heavy cinematic drums or engine roars.

38:34.280 --> 38:38.900
Three is spectral harshness, like screeching tires or breaking glass.

38:39.200 --> 38:41.060
And four is volume swells.

38:41.060 --> 38:43.080
Okay, so it's measuring all those at once.

38:43.880 --> 38:50.800
Overdetermination occurs when multiple or all of these salience channels spike at the exact same time.

38:50.800 --> 38:59.660
The film isn't just loud, it is redundantly screaming for your attention using every acoustic tool available in the arsenal simultaneously.

38:59.880 --> 39:03.320
Okay, I am going to play devil's advocate here and push back on that.

39:03.380 --> 39:03.840
Go for it.

39:03.840 --> 39:20.540
Because if a scene features a massive car bomb exploding, a character screaming in terror, a booming cinematic drum hit in the musical score, and the harsh sound of a skyscraper's glass shattering all at once, is that actually bad filmmaking?

39:20.660 --> 39:21.700
It's a fair question.

39:21.700 --> 39:24.780
Or is that just the necessary grammar of an action climax?

39:25.480 --> 39:31.920
It feels to me like the director is pulling every fire alarm in the building at the exact same time to make sure the audience knows there's a fire.

39:32.320 --> 39:36.800
Doesn't an action movie need to be overwhelming in those moments to deliver on its genre promise?

39:37.220 --> 39:43.700
That is a very fair critique, and the author actually acknowledges that redundancy is a standard tool for making a signal robust.

39:44.640 --> 39:48.800
It's not inherently wrong to use every tool at once for a massive explosion.

39:48.800 --> 39:51.720
The problem is not that overdetermination happens.

39:52.320 --> 39:56.340
The problem is when overdetermination is sustained continuously over time.

39:56.460 --> 39:58.340
Ah, the 12 minutes.

39:58.460 --> 39:58.860
Exactly.

39:59.720 --> 40:02.520
This brings us to the concept of recovery debt.

40:03.020 --> 40:09.580
This is the physiological and psychological toll that builds up in the viewer when the film refuses to return to the baseline.

40:09.840 --> 40:12.880
So it's not the alarm itself, it's that it never stops ringing.

40:13.280 --> 40:17.600
When a film pulls every single fire alarm in the building, that's fine for 10 seconds.

40:17.600 --> 40:24.500
But when it leaves every alarm ringing for 12 straight minutes, it forces the viewer into a state of cognitive overload.

40:25.120 --> 40:30.400
It locks them in what the acoustic ecologist R. Murray Schaefer calls a lo-fi soundscape.

40:31.000 --> 40:33.480
What is the difference between a hi-fi and a lo-fi soundscape?

40:33.580 --> 40:36.820
In a hi-fi soundscape, the signal-to-noise ratio is favorable.

40:37.500 --> 40:39.900
Discrete sounds are heard clearly against a quiet background.

40:40.360 --> 40:43.660
You can locate a bird chirping in the trees or a footstep behind you.

40:43.920 --> 40:45.300
Perspective and space exist.

40:45.620 --> 40:46.900
In a lo-fi soundscape.

40:46.900 --> 40:51.560
In a lo-fi soundscape, there's so much broadband noise that signals crowd each other out.

40:52.120 --> 40:53.740
Perspective is completely destroyed.

40:54.060 --> 40:56.360
The loudest sound masks everything else.

40:56.500 --> 40:58.580
So everything is just competing to be the loudest.

40:58.700 --> 41:05.200
During that final 20-minute stretch of drive-through fire, the data proves the viewer is trapped in a lo-fi environment.

41:05.740 --> 41:11.060
The discrete sounds of the action crowd into a muddy mass, the recovery debt skyrockets,

41:11.060 --> 41:15.100
and the listener's brain is given no off-ramp to process the information.

41:15.720 --> 41:22.840
So the data absolutely proves that the movie is screaming at the viewer without pause for over a decade in movie time.

41:22.960 --> 41:23.520
It does.

41:23.520 --> 41:26.000
But here is the trickiest part of the whole paper for me.

41:26.460 --> 41:30.000
How do we scientifically measure the viewer screaming back?

41:30.080 --> 41:30.360
Right.

41:30.700 --> 41:32.620
How do we measure the human response?

41:32.860 --> 41:37.380
Because the listener claiming to their friend that they muted the TV is just an anecdote.

41:38.060 --> 41:39.380
Human memory is terrible.

41:39.500 --> 41:40.360
We exaggerate.

41:40.360 --> 41:45.300
How does Flyxion turn human annoyance into hard, irrefutable data?

41:45.660 --> 41:49.280
This is perhaps the most innovative and exciting part of the entire manuscript.

41:50.200 --> 41:53.800
Flyxion proposes an experimental software tool called MuteNet.

41:54.060 --> 41:54.560
MuteNet.

41:54.840 --> 41:56.080
Sounds like a sci-fi villain.

41:56.380 --> 41:56.860
It does.

41:56.960 --> 42:04.740
It relies on a custom script written in a programming language called Lua, specifically throttle-log.lua.

42:05.380 --> 42:12.000
This script is designed to run silently in the background of an open-source desktop media player called MPV.

42:12.200 --> 42:12.460
Wait.

42:13.000 --> 42:16.420
A script running in the background tracking every time I touch my volume keys.

42:16.540 --> 42:19.640
I know we're doing science here, but that sounds borderline dystopian.

42:19.720 --> 42:22.380
Are we talking about media players spying on us to see how we watch movies?

42:22.380 --> 42:24.040
It is a valid concern, but no.

42:24.120 --> 42:27.780
This is strictly a local, opt-in, experimental setup.

42:28.140 --> 42:29.700
Nothing is being broadcast to a server.

42:29.800 --> 42:30.780
Okay, that's a relief.

42:31.160 --> 42:34.080
But what it does locally is incredibly powerful.

42:34.080 --> 42:39.960
The script silently logs an event every single time the user touches the volume up or down

42:39.960 --> 42:43.680
control, mutes the audio entirely, or hits the pause button.

42:43.760 --> 42:45.920
So it captures every single micro-adjustment.

42:46.000 --> 42:51.760
It records the exact wall clock time in the real world, the exact movie time stamp down

42:51.760 --> 42:56.460
to the millisecond, the digital slider value of the volume, and the corresponding game

42:56.460 --> 42:57.320
in decibels.

42:57.420 --> 43:02.600
So it creates an exact, millisecond-accurate map of human behavior synced perfectly to the

43:02.600 --> 43:03.300
movie's timeline.

43:03.300 --> 43:04.260
Precisely.

43:04.800 --> 43:09.820
This allows the researcher to calculate a new metric they call implied throttle depth.

43:10.180 --> 43:11.380
Implied throttle depth.

43:11.420 --> 43:12.140
What does that measure?

43:12.320 --> 43:13.960
It measures the amount of attenuation.

43:14.360 --> 43:19.580
So the exact number of decibels you turned the volume down by that was required to hold

43:19.580 --> 43:22.940
a loud block of audio at a level you found tolerable.

43:23.180 --> 43:23.380
Okay.

43:23.380 --> 43:29.060
By overlaying the user's volume logs onto the Python audio analysis we talked about earlier,

43:29.620 --> 43:34.720
Flyxion can see exactly which mathematical acoustic events trigger a human being to reach

43:34.720 --> 43:35.300
for the remote.

43:35.300 --> 43:37.620
That is brilliant methodology.

43:38.020 --> 43:41.940
It completely removes the unreliability of human memory from the equation.

43:42.020 --> 43:42.360
Exactly.

43:42.480 --> 43:43.300
You don't have to ask them.

43:43.460 --> 43:44.780
You don't have to ask a test subject.

43:44.900 --> 43:46.880
Hey, did you think that helicopter scene was too loud?

43:47.320 --> 43:54.300
You just look at the throttle-log.lua data and see that at exactly one hour, 12 minutes,

43:54.300 --> 43:59.920
and four seconds, right as the overdetermination peaked, they panicked and dropped the volume

43:59.920 --> 44:01.080
by 40 decibels.

44:01.480 --> 44:01.780
Yes.

44:02.220 --> 44:06.000
And this data reveals something profound about the psychology of the home viewer.

44:06.420 --> 44:08.180
This raises an important question.

44:08.720 --> 44:15.180
And there is a landmark 1972 community noise study conducted by two researchers named Glass

44:15.180 --> 44:17.640
and Singer that is highly relevant here.

44:17.900 --> 44:20.440
What did Glass and Singer discover about noise?

44:20.440 --> 44:25.780
They were studying urban noise pollution, they put test subjects in a room to perform

44:25.780 --> 44:29.240
complex cognitive puzzles, and subjected them to...

44:29.240 --> 44:29.760
Oh, wow.

44:30.520 --> 44:34.560
Just knowing the shield is there protects you, even if you don't raise it.

44:34.700 --> 44:35.220
Precisely.

44:35.460 --> 44:39.580
So what does this mean for the listener on the couch watching Drive Thru Fire?

44:40.140 --> 44:45.520
It means that your hand hovering over the mute button isn't just a sign of you being a passive,

44:45.720 --> 44:46.560
annoyed consumer.

44:46.560 --> 44:53.380
You are actively engaging in what the theorist Steve Goodman calls sonic warfare with the

44:53.380 --> 44:53.780
director.

44:54.120 --> 44:54.440
That's right.

44:54.820 --> 45:00.080
You are using the television remote as a psychological shield to protect your cognitive state.

45:00.140 --> 45:00.580
Exactly.

45:01.000 --> 45:05.300
Turning the volume down is not just about protecting your eardrums from physical damage.

45:05.800 --> 45:11.280
It is a measurable behavioral act of regaining control over an invasive, effective weapon.

45:11.440 --> 45:11.840
Wow.

45:12.260 --> 45:13.160
Sonic warfare.

45:13.160 --> 45:17.600
The movie is trying to conquer your living room, and the volume knob is your instrument

45:17.600 --> 45:18.320
of defense.

45:19.080 --> 45:24.180
But, as Flictiona notes, to ensure this mute net data is scientifically bulletproof, you

45:24.180 --> 45:26.260
cannot just rely on one listener's log.

45:26.360 --> 45:27.520
You have to validate it.

45:27.660 --> 45:31.680
How do you validate a subjective feeling of annoyance across multiple people?

45:31.980 --> 45:35.060
Through blind, multi-listener annotations.

45:35.060 --> 45:44.720
You take the mute net software and have it automatically extract short clips of the movie that scored the highest in harshness or over-determination.

45:45.080 --> 45:46.580
Okay, taking the worst moments.

45:46.760 --> 45:53.020
Then, you mix those intense clips up with random control clips taken from quiet, conversational

45:53.020 --> 45:53.800
parts of the movie.

45:54.180 --> 45:59.020
You present this randomized playlist to a large group of listeners who have no idea what the

45:59.020 --> 46:00.120
algorithm scored them.

46:00.200 --> 46:01.860
So, listeners are going in totally blind.

46:01.940 --> 46:03.600
They don't know what the computer thinks is loud.

46:03.600 --> 46:04.000
Right.

46:04.520 --> 46:08.320
This ensures the human listeners don't just validate the algorithm's bias.

46:08.860 --> 46:14.760
The humans have to independently prove that the exact moments the computer flagged as mathematically

46:14.760 --> 46:20.440
unbearable are, in fact, the exact moments human beings reach for the volume shield.

46:20.580 --> 46:21.540
Okay, so let's zoom out.

46:21.900 --> 46:23.180
The data pipeline is solid.

46:23.600 --> 46:24.900
The instruments are calibrated.

46:25.340 --> 46:28.040
We have proven mathematically that the mix is oppressive.

46:28.040 --> 46:33.240
And we have proven behaviorally that viewers are out there frantically butchering the film's

46:33.240 --> 46:36.760
audio with their remotes just to survive the third act.

46:36.820 --> 46:37.700
We have proven the disease.

46:37.800 --> 46:39.140
We know the problem intimately.

46:39.740 --> 46:43.580
But is there a more elegant way to save the movie watching experience?

46:44.300 --> 46:49.940
If we can scientifically identify the disease of occupation, can we engineer a cure?

46:50.300 --> 46:50.760
We can.

46:50.760 --> 46:56.880
In the final section of the manuscript, Flyxion outlines a fascinating, highly technical intervention.

46:57.300 --> 46:58.000
Tell me about it.

46:58.320 --> 47:03.720
Using those same FFmpeg audio tools, they propose creating what they call a dialogue-forward

47:03.720 --> 47:04.460
downmix.

47:04.880 --> 47:09.940
It is essentially an automated, intelligent, mathematically precise remix of the film,

47:10.420 --> 47:12.860
custom-built for the uncalibrated home environment.

47:13.220 --> 47:14.240
Break this down for me.

47:14.400 --> 47:17.540
How does this automated remix actually work under the hood?

47:17.540 --> 47:19.760
What is the code doing to the audio file?

47:20.000 --> 47:21.840
It executes a sequence of critical interventions.

47:22.360 --> 47:27.580
First, it looks at the original 5.1 surround sound mix and deliberately extracts the center

47:27.580 --> 47:31.720
channel, which, as we discussed earlier, is where the vast majority of the human dialogue

47:31.720 --> 47:32.120
lives.

47:32.180 --> 47:32.340
Right.

47:32.420 --> 47:33.260
The actor's talking.

47:33.420 --> 47:38.760
It then pans that center channel forward, artificially raising its volume level relative to the left,

47:38.880 --> 47:40.020
right, and surround channels.

47:40.300 --> 47:45.000
It forcefully restores the vococentric balance that the bass amp or the cheap soundbar tries

47:45.000 --> 47:45.660
to destroy.

47:45.660 --> 47:50.440
So, step one is basically giving the actors a megaphone so they can be heard over the

47:50.440 --> 47:50.940
explosions.

47:51.340 --> 47:52.020
What's step two?

47:52.260 --> 47:57.320
Step two is applying aggressive dynamic range compression to the entire track.

47:57.460 --> 47:57.780
Okay.

47:57.980 --> 47:58.680
Compression again.

47:58.880 --> 48:02.320
This is where we get into some heavy audio engineering settings.

48:03.120 --> 48:09.100
The paper proposes a specific compressor ratio of six to one, an incredibly fast attack time

48:09.100 --> 48:13.160
of five milliseconds, and a release time of 250 milliseconds.

48:13.160 --> 48:15.940
Okay, you're going to have to translate that audio engineering jargon for me.

48:16.020 --> 48:17.720
I know what a compressor broadly does.

48:17.860 --> 48:19.300
It squashes volume.

48:19.960 --> 48:25.200
But what does a five millisecond attack and a 250 millisecond release actually sound like

48:25.200 --> 48:25.600
physically?

48:25.960 --> 48:30.140
Think of a compressor as an invisible hand resting on the volume dial, waiting for the

48:30.140 --> 48:31.180
sound to get too loud.

48:31.300 --> 48:31.560
Okay.

48:31.700 --> 48:32.460
I like that visual.

48:32.560 --> 48:35.140
The attack time is a measure of reflexes.

48:35.360 --> 48:40.440
It dictates how fast that invisible hand yanks the volume down once a loud noise crosses the

48:40.440 --> 48:40.900
threshold.

48:40.900 --> 48:42.160
Ah, reflexes.

48:42.240 --> 48:42.560
Got it.

48:42.960 --> 48:45.360
Five milliseconds is practically instantaneous.

48:46.040 --> 48:49.980
It means that the split second, a gunshot or an explosion erupts.

48:50.420 --> 48:55.520
The compressor grabs the audio and squashes it down before the sound wave can even fully

48:55.520 --> 48:56.820
develop and hit your ears.

48:57.060 --> 49:00.400
It neutralizes the transient impact immediately.

49:00.600 --> 49:01.380
Okay, that makes sense.

49:01.480 --> 49:03.660
Fast attack stops the jump scare.

49:03.880 --> 49:04.680
What is the release?

49:04.680 --> 49:10.360
The release time is how long the invisible hand holds onto the volume dial before slowly

49:10.360 --> 49:11.800
letting it back up to normal.

49:11.940 --> 49:14.260
And they set it to 250 milliseconds.

49:14.520 --> 49:14.880
Yes.

49:15.520 --> 49:17.840
250 milliseconds is a quarter of a second.

49:18.080 --> 49:19.380
That is very intentional.

49:19.920 --> 49:24.640
It is fast enough that the volume returns to a normal level before the next line of dialogue

49:24.640 --> 49:25.220
is spoken.

49:25.380 --> 49:26.180
Oh, that's crucial.

49:26.180 --> 49:31.000
If the release is too slow, the explosion happens, the volume drops, and the next line

49:31.000 --> 49:33.760
of dialogue gets squashed too, making it impossible to hear.

49:33.920 --> 49:34.040
Right.

49:34.120 --> 49:39.360
If it's too fast, you get a weird, distracting, pumping sound where the audio sounds like it

49:39.360 --> 49:40.520
is hyperventilating.

49:40.520 --> 49:43.280
250 milliseconds is the sweet spot.

49:43.380 --> 49:44.760
Okay, that makes perfect sense.

49:45.200 --> 49:49.380
Finally, after the compression, the script applies a hard limiter ceiling.

49:50.080 --> 49:56.880
This is an absolute mathematical brick wall that the audio simply cannot exceed, guaranteeing

49:56.880 --> 50:00.980
that the signal will never clip your amplifier and create that harsh distortion we talked

50:00.980 --> 50:01.520
about earlier.

50:01.760 --> 50:03.280
That sounds like a dream come true.

50:03.700 --> 50:07.460
A movie that just plays at a comfortable level where I'm going to hear the dialogue and the

50:07.460 --> 50:09.080
explosions don't wake up my kids.

50:09.080 --> 50:10.720
It solves the problem completely.

50:11.060 --> 50:15.480
But I can already hear the film purists and the Hollywood sound mixers screaming in the

50:15.480 --> 50:15.820
distance.

50:16.240 --> 50:19.060
This destroys the filmmakers' artistic intent.

50:19.180 --> 50:20.920
Oh, they definitely hate this idea.

50:21.080 --> 50:21.320
Right.

50:21.440 --> 50:27.400
They want you to experience the vast, earth-shattering, dynamic range exactly as the director mixed

50:27.400 --> 50:27.640
it.

50:27.860 --> 50:30.600
Isn't this script basically vandalizing their art?

50:30.940 --> 50:34.580
The paper anticipates the exact, highly emotional objection.

50:34.580 --> 50:40.440
And Flitchie and his counter-argument to the artistic intent crowd is devastatingly logical.

50:40.660 --> 50:41.160
Let's hear it.

50:41.680 --> 50:47.560
The dynamic relationships of the original, pristine mix were authored for a rigorously calibrated

50:47.560 --> 50:51.600
room operating with massive headroom at 85 decibels.

50:51.780 --> 50:53.040
Like we talked about with the theaters.

50:53.540 --> 50:54.180
Exactly.

50:54.180 --> 51:00.940
In your uncalibrated living room, playing through a plastic soundbar at a much lower volume, those

51:00.940 --> 51:03.680
delicate acoustic relationships are already dead.

51:04.180 --> 51:07.760
The authored mix simply does not survive the journey to your couch.

51:08.160 --> 51:09.460
Let me try an analogy.

51:09.460 --> 51:15.900
It's definitely like a world-class chef spending hours meticulously balancing the delicate flavors

51:15.900 --> 51:18.420
of a dish in a pristine kitchen.

51:18.560 --> 51:18.920
Right.

51:19.100 --> 51:23.940
But then, the waiter serves it to you on a paper plate, outside, in the middle of a hurricane.

51:24.480 --> 51:28.500
The environment has already fundamentally ruined the delicate balance of the food.

51:28.580 --> 51:29.880
It's completely lost.

51:30.260 --> 51:33.120
The dialogue forward down mix isn't an insult to the chef.

51:33.540 --> 51:34.900
We aren't disrespecting the art.

51:35.100 --> 51:38.680
It's just us trying to salvage the protein from the storm so we don't go hungry.

51:38.680 --> 51:40.700
That is a brilliant analogy.

51:41.120 --> 51:45.080
The author points out that the user frantically jabbing the mute button on their remote for

51:45.080 --> 51:48.680
two straight minutes is the crudest possible remix anyway.

51:48.780 --> 51:48.980
Right.

51:49.080 --> 51:50.180
I'm remixing it manually.

51:50.480 --> 51:50.900
Badly.

51:51.140 --> 51:53.160
The purest argument presents a false choice.

51:53.820 --> 51:57.860
The choice isn't between the pristine artistic intent and an altered mix.

51:58.300 --> 52:03.880
The choice is between the viewer randomly chopping the volume to zero, entirely losing all the

52:03.880 --> 52:08.540
dialogue and the score, versus a design mathematical alteration that keeps the dialogue

52:08.540 --> 52:13.600
intelligible, while bringing the explosions into a physical range the living room can actually

52:13.600 --> 52:14.080
handle.

52:14.320 --> 52:17.320
It seems like there's a whole spectrum of how we can fix this.

52:17.780 --> 52:19.820
It's a continuum of interventions.

52:20.440 --> 52:26.060
On the simplest end, you have the basic night mode, or speech enhancement toggle, that comes

52:26.060 --> 52:27.620
built into most modern TVs.

52:28.000 --> 52:30.060
Which is a very blunt instrument, but it helps.

52:30.180 --> 52:30.360
Right.

52:30.360 --> 52:33.940
In the middle, you have Flyxion's channel rebalancing script.

52:34.340 --> 52:38.740
But looking forward, this stretches all the way into the realm of artificial intelligence

52:38.740 --> 52:39.720
and source separation.

52:39.920 --> 52:41.200
This is where it gets really exciting.

52:41.620 --> 52:46.480
Imagine an AI processor in your TV that doesn't just apply a blanket compressor to lower the

52:46.480 --> 52:46.960
explosions.

52:47.340 --> 52:53.000
Imagine an AI that actually analyzes the audio in real time, identifies the specific acoustic

52:53.000 --> 52:59.180
signature of an automatic weapon, and seamlessly replaces it with a softer, stylized, less

52:59.180 --> 53:00.000
abrasive thud.

53:00.240 --> 53:02.820
It could isolate specific frequencies entirely.

53:03.080 --> 53:08.660
That could be life-changing for neurodivergent viewers who suffer from sensory overload, but

53:08.660 --> 53:10.480
still want to enjoy an action movie.

53:10.700 --> 53:16.320
That forward-looking vision is exactly the ultimate benchmark for success that Flyxion proposes

53:16.320 --> 53:18.240
in Hypothesis 5 of the paper.

53:18.600 --> 53:19.720
What's Hypothesis 5?

53:19.720 --> 53:25.020
How do we know when an intervention, whether it's a simple compressor or an advanced AI,

53:25.240 --> 53:26.360
is actually successful?

53:27.080 --> 53:33.000
An intervention is only successful if it can be proven via that mutinette logging data to

53:33.000 --> 53:37.720
significantly reduce the number of times the user desperately reaches for their remote

53:37.720 --> 53:42.060
control without causing any loss in their comprehension of the dialogue.

53:42.740 --> 53:43.960
So if they don't touch the remote?

53:44.140 --> 53:48.080
If they don't have to touch the remote for the entire two hours and they can still accurately

53:48.080 --> 53:52.060
tell you the plot of the film, the intervention was a scientific success.

53:52.460 --> 53:56.800
So after digging through all this math, all the Python scripts, the acoustic theory, and

53:56.800 --> 54:00.340
the hardware failures, what is the ultimate takeaway here?

54:00.420 --> 54:02.480
To me, it's a massive reframing of the problem.

54:02.640 --> 54:03.700
It absolutely is.

54:03.700 --> 54:07.180
The true measure of a bad home theater mix isn't just loudness.

54:07.820 --> 54:09.440
Loudness is a momentary spike.

54:10.160 --> 54:15.420
The real enemy, the thing that is actually ruining our movie nights, is the relentless,

54:16.020 --> 54:18.660
inescapable occupation of our auditory space.

54:18.840 --> 54:20.220
And the denial of recovery.

54:20.580 --> 54:22.000
It is the denial of recovery.

54:22.680 --> 54:28.540
Flixn proved that by looking past the simple, lazy averages of LUFS, and by being willing to

54:28.540 --> 54:33.280
admit when their own data instruments were producing confident nonsense, we can actually

54:33.280 --> 54:35.660
objectively measure viewer fatigue.

54:36.240 --> 54:38.740
We can map the physical exhaustion of our ears.

54:39.060 --> 54:43.900
We can rigorously quantify the mechanical and acoustic failure of the translation between

54:43.900 --> 54:45.100
the cinema and the living room.

54:45.420 --> 54:49.440
It takes the blame off the viewer and places it on the uncalibrated environment.

54:49.600 --> 54:50.220
I love that.

54:50.580 --> 54:54.240
So the next time you are sitting on your couch in the dark and you find your thumb furiously

54:54.240 --> 54:58.440
jabbing the volume down button during the climax of an action movie, take a deep breath.

54:58.560 --> 54:59.460
You are not crazy.

54:59.600 --> 55:00.460
You are not crazy.

55:00.560 --> 55:01.760
You are not overly sensitive.

55:01.960 --> 55:06.480
You are simply a human being reacting naturally to a hostile lo-fi soundscape.

55:06.820 --> 55:12.500
You are generating a vital behavioral data point in the ongoing invisible war for acoustic

55:12.500 --> 55:13.000
recovery.

55:13.000 --> 55:18.180
And if tools like Mutant eventually make it out into the wild, moving beyond experimental

55:18.180 --> 55:23.580
research to aggregate millions of these viewer volume adjustments across the globe.

55:23.580 --> 55:24.960
Imagine the data.

55:25.220 --> 55:25.440
Right.

